Trading hiatus — deliberate halt of the LIVE instance.

Since:   2026-06 (owner decision; confirmed 2026-07-15 during the fresh-eyes
         audit). Moved from the repo-root HALT file to this per-instance
         sentinel 2026-09-22 as part of the instance genericisation cutover
         (PR #121).

Why:     The live Alpaca account was fully liquidated — positions closed, cash
         withdrawn, account inert. Economics are under review (audit finding
         0.2: lifetime gross trading P&L ~= -$35 against ~$150-250 of API
         spend), and the Tier 1 money-path fixes were prerequisites for
         re-enabling the cron.

Effect:  cron-wrap.sh (invoked with --instance live) and run-docker.sh skip
         any live-instance job while this file exists, exiting 0 — a
         deliberate halt is not a failure. This file halts ONLY the live
         instance: paper keeps trading, with no --ignore-halt workaround
         needed (that flag is again reserved for protect-the-data jobs like
         backups). To halt everything on this host regardless of instance,
         `touch HALT` in the repo root. To halt paper alone,
         `touch instances/paper.HALT`.

         In-container twin: ALGO_TRADING_HALTED in instances/live.env —
         covers `task session INSTANCE=live` and manual invocations, but
         needs a container recreate on a long-running stack; this sentinel
         is the no-restart path.

         A halted job still sends its dead-man's-switch ping. The switch
         answers "is this host up and is cron firing", and during a
         deliberate hiatus the answer is yes — going quiet on purpose must
         not look identical to going quiet by accident.

         The nightly backups run with --ignore-halt and continue through the
         hiatus.

History: Between 2026-08-13 and 2026-09-22 the halt was a repo-root HALT file
         that covered every job, and paper's exemption was --ignore-halt on
         its crontab line — a misuse of a flag meant for backups, replaced by
         this per-instance model. Before 2026-08 the halt lived only in a
         hand-edited installed crontab — uncommitted, undated, and identical
         in appearance to a system that had silently stalled. That ambiguity
         is the thing these sentinel files exist to remove.

Resume:  docs/runbook-recovery.md, "Halt / Resume". Short version: review the
         audit's Tier 1 / 0.2 status, `git rm instances/live.HALT`, clear any
         ALGO_TRADING_HALTED in instances/live.env, uncomment the live
         session lines in `crontab`, reinstall with
         `crontab /home/jay/dev/algo/crontab`, and commit — a resume should
         be as visible in git as the halt.

         Note: re-depositing capital will read as a giant equity jump to the
         dashboard and the reflection stage, which cannot distinguish
         deposits from performance (audit B.6/2.6).
